WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:01.580
Film Courage: What is the first step in writing
a screenplay?

00:00:01.580 --> 00:00:06.259
Richard Walter: Getting your butt in the chair
and hands on the keys and seeing where it

00:00:06.259 --> 00:00:07.310
goes.

00:00:07.310 --> 00:00:15.660
I believe you have to have an outline, but
then you have to throw away that outline.

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:23.720
It will take paths that are surprises to the
artist that created it.

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:33.470
I’ve never known anyone who wasn’t surprised
by lines of dialogue that characters seem

00:00:33.470 --> 00:00:37.150
to invent by themselves, twists and turns
in the story.

00:00:37.150 --> 00:00:40.310
I remember asking Neil Simon “Do you laugh
at your own jokes?”

00:00:40.310 --> 00:00:44.570
And he said “Sure I do, the first time I
hear them.”

00:00:44.570 --> 00:00:47.140
And I think that’s fantastic that he actually
hears them.

00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:51.120
It’s as if someone else is telling them…telling
these jokes to him.

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:55.070
And that’s the experience of a lot of artists.

00:00:55.070 --> 00:01:03.230
I wrote…I sold a TWILIGHT ZONE episode years
and years go about a muse, some sort of composer

00:01:03.230 --> 00:01:09.470
of commercial jingles and his muse that proposes…I’ll
spare you the story.

00:01:09.470 --> 00:01:13.320
If I told you the story you’d think “Hey,
what a good story!”

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:19.160
But the thesis underneath it was the notion
that muses don’t desert their artists.

00:01:19.160 --> 00:01:21.110
It’s really the other way around.

00:01:21.110 --> 00:01:22.590
You have to be available.

00:01:22.590 --> 00:01:27.310
It’s never easy to get into.

00:01:27.310 --> 00:01:28.310
And you have to own that.

00:01:28.310 --> 00:01:30.630
And you have to know that.

00:01:30.630 --> 00:01:32.520
Not sure that answers What is the First Thing
You Do?

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:38.420
Usually something occurs to you that seems
kind of odd.

00:01:38.420 --> 00:01:40.720
Imagined if this happened or that happened?

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:49.750
I remember when our first child was going
to be born we went to birth preparation classes

00:01:49.750 --> 00:01:53.920
which were held at Cedars Sinai.

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:56.390
And everybody was supposed to bring a pillow.

00:01:56.390 --> 00:01:59.950
So suddenly outside you know, Beverly Blvd.

00:01:59.950 --> 00:02:09.390
near San Vincent [in Los Angeles] all of these
couples, all of the women are quite pregnant

00:02:09.390 --> 00:02:14.440
and everybody is carrying pillows “What’s
that about?”

00:02:14.440 --> 00:02:20.281
Imagine you drive past the bus stop and there
are like 60 people at the bus stop and they

00:02:20.281 --> 00:02:21.281
all have accordions.

00:02:21.281 --> 00:02:24.080
I don’t know they all have accordions for
some reason.

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:25.610
“What could that be?”

00:02:25.610 --> 00:02:30.450
You start to think about that and also you
don’t think about it you just sort of let

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:35.440
it simmer and cook and the notion will come
to you.

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:41.420
And you start to play with it and see how
it unfolds.

00:02:41.420 --> 00:02:46.290
I know there are other people, including people
you are talking to I’m sure, who have much

00:02:46.290 --> 00:02:48.320
more precise steps.

00:02:48.320 --> 00:02:54.730
Well I reject that in my own work and in the
writers that I know.

00:02:54.730 --> 00:03:03.030
Again, I think that it’s a function of surprise,
a lot of art is.

00:03:03.030 --> 00:03:07.770
And the important thing for the artist is
to stay open to those surprises rather than

00:03:07.770 --> 00:03:13.840
trying to drag the narrative back to some
previous intellectual perception that you

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:14.840
had.

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:19.051
The very first script I ever wrote and I never
did sell it (I wrote it in class) like my

00:03:19.051 --> 00:03:26.390
own class, except it was cross-town it was
Irwin R. Blacker, legendary teacher of screenwriting,

00:03:26.390 --> 00:03:32.450
now long deceased, but he taught George and
Milius and all these people.

00:03:32.450 --> 00:03:45.040
And I wrote a story based loosely on a when
I worked on the project, the pilot HEAD START

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:51.280
program, the war on poverty Lyndon B. Johnson
program having to do with support for pre-schoolers

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:52.280
in the schools.

00:03:52.280 --> 00:04:01.272
And there was one social worker (a white guy)
advantaged, privileged guy, but he would kind

00:04:01.272 --> 00:04:07.150
of speak with kind of a fake black accent,
this street-ey kind of black jive “Hey Bro…wuz

00:04:07.150 --> 00:04:13.690
goin’ on?” and he thought that really
impressed the black kids he was working with.

00:04:13.690 --> 00:04:14.750
He was not working with preschoolers.

00:04:14.750 --> 00:04:19.139
He had started working with adolescents in
another program.

00:04:19.139 --> 00:04:24.029
And the establishment, the people who ran
that program thought “Boy this guy really

00:04:24.029 --> 00:04:25.029
goes the extra mile!”

00:04:25.029 --> 00:04:26.029
I thought he was an idiot!

00:04:26.029 --> 00:04:29.080
That he’s just patronizing and condescending.

00:04:29.080 --> 00:04:30.360
You know, I’m a jew.

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:34.360
Imagine someone trying to make points with
me in a Yiddish Accent “Richard….”

00:04:34.360 --> 00:04:35.800
[imitates a heavy Yiddish accent].

00:04:35.800 --> 00:04:38.050
“What a cool guy this is.

00:04:38.050 --> 00:04:39.280
He talks the talk!”

00:04:39.280 --> 00:04:42.460
Well, this guy got killed.

00:04:42.460 --> 00:04:47.050
He was murdered and I thought well that is
something to write a script about.

00:04:47.050 --> 00:04:52.150
When I wrote the script, I thought it was
the social worker’s story (the white guy’s

00:04:52.150 --> 00:04:53.150
story).

00:04:53.150 --> 00:04:56.139
But when I was done, it was really the black
kids story and I didn’t realize this until

00:04:56.139 --> 00:04:58.479
I was done with the first draft.

00:04:58.479 --> 00:05:01.669
And the smartest thing I did was to leave
it alone in that regard.

00:05:01.669 --> 00:05:06.490
That is to say I rewrote it several times
but I let it become the black kids story,

00:05:06.490 --> 00:05:08.810
which is a much more interesting story.

00:05:08.810 --> 00:05:15.379
The white guy was a subsidiary character now,
not the protagonist of the piece.

00:05:15.379 --> 00:05:18.659
I never sold that script but I did get top
representation.

00:05:18.659 --> 00:05:19.900
I got assignments.

00:05:19.900 --> 00:05:22.431
I got on staff at Universal [Pictures].

00:05:22.431 --> 00:05:25.349
A lot of writers don’t get it that when
a script doesn’t sell that’s not the end,

00:05:25.349 --> 00:05:26.349
it’s just the beginning.

00:05:26.349 --> 00:05:30.090
There are all kinds of rewards that can flow
from a script that doesn’t sell.

00:05:30.090 --> 00:05:34.270
Also a script that doesn’t sell now might
sell you know, down the line.

00:05:34.270 --> 00:05:41.810
Clint Eastwood made UNFORGIVEN which won the
Oscar for best screenplay, best movie 20 years

00:05:41.810 --> 00:05:45.169
after he acquired that script.

00:05:45.169 --> 00:05:50.499
So you never really know.

00:05:50.499 --> 00:06:00.249
Again, if all the work you put into the first
draft and you end up realizing that the guy

00:06:00.249 --> 00:06:03.759
you thought was the protagonist was not the
protagonist (it’s not his story at all),

00:06:03.759 --> 00:06:05.580
that’s not a waste.

00:06:05.580 --> 00:06:06.650
That shouldn’t be frustrating.

00:06:06.650 --> 00:06:08.879
That’s a really good use of that draft.

00:06:08.879 --> 00:06:13.379
You’ll salvage some of the stuff in the
draft but also you’ll have used it to point

00:06:13.379 --> 00:06:15.610
you in the direction that you need to go.

00:06:15.610 --> 00:06:18.740
You can’t figure it out in advance, you
just can’t.

00:06:18.740 --> 00:06:21.619
Kushner at the LINCOLN screening…have you
seen it?

00:06:21.619 --> 00:06:27.770
Maybe you won’t like it at all but it’s
impossible not to be astonished at how important

00:06:27.770 --> 00:06:35.240
it is and engaging it is in the best sense
of important.

00:06:35.240 --> 00:06:38.479
And it is a stupendous screenplay by Kushner.

00:06:38.479 --> 00:06:43.369
And Tony (Kushner) was saying “A lot of
people think you think the thing up and you

00:06:43.369 --> 00:06:49.759
write it down but the writing down of it is
sort of the thinking of it.

00:06:49.759 --> 00:06:56.409
There is a nexus between the pen or the keyboard,
or the hands on the keyboard that you just

00:06:56.409 --> 00:06:59.400
never know and you have to live with that
uncertainty.

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:07.110
You have to sort of rejoice and celebrate
and face that uncertainty instead of trying

00:07:07.110 --> 00:07:09.279
to eliminate the uncertainty.

00:07:09.279 --> 00:07:13.809
You look at the studios today and what is
less interesting than what they are doing

00:07:13.809 --> 00:07:14.809
now?

00:07:14.809 --> 00:07:18.349
They are doing prequels and sequels, items
of franchises.

00:07:18.349 --> 00:07:21.080
What they are trying to do is minimize risk.

00:07:21.080 --> 00:07:27.970
And they are trying to make it so when an
audience goes to see a movie they get what

00:07:27.970 --> 00:07:28.970
they expect.

00:07:28.970 --> 00:07:31.969
Well when I go see a movie, I don’t want
to see what I expected.

00:07:31.969 --> 00:07:34.419
My expectations should be exceeded.

00:07:34.419 --> 00:07:36.770
I want to be turned upside down.

00:07:36.770 --> 00:07:39.189
I want to be frightened.

00:07:39.189 --> 00:07:41.800
I want life to be changed forever.

00:07:41.800 --> 00:07:51.999
It’s funny, I lectured in September a year
ago to an evangelical Christian conference

00:07:51.999 --> 00:07:58.979
in Chicago, 500 passengers all across the
nation on narrative and scripture.

00:07:58.979 --> 00:08:04.610
I was never more warmly greeted or generously
received.

00:08:04.610 --> 00:08:06.659
They also payed me very well.

00:08:06.659 --> 00:08:09.300
But I never had a better time, you know.

00:08:09.300 --> 00:08:15.649
I’ve given hundreds of speeches all around
the world and I never had a better time than

00:08:15.649 --> 00:08:18.659
I had with all these sweet Christians.

00:08:18.659 --> 00:08:23.779
And one of the things I told him was if you
wanted to keep people in the Church even after

00:08:23.779 --> 00:08:29.499
they leave the Church on Sunday morning…that
is to say…thinking about the sermon just

00:08:29.499 --> 00:08:35.410
like after when they leave your movie…I’m
still thinking about LINCOLN and I saw it

00:08:35.410 --> 00:08:40.060
over a week ago and it’s still playing in
my head.

00:08:40.060 --> 00:08:43.310
And the more time that passes, the more I’m
into it.

00:08:43.310 --> 00:08:45.520
It’s not fading, it’s getting stronger.

00:08:45.520 --> 00:08:59.580
Maybe I’ll go see it again?

00:08:59.580 --> 00:09:06.100
If you want people to stay in the movie, just
like if you want them to stay in the Church,

00:09:06.100 --> 00:09:10.640
what I told them was you don’t need to make
people feel good, you just have to make people

00:09:10.640 --> 00:09:11.640
feel.

00:09:11.640 --> 00:09:14.110
Scare them half to death.

00:09:14.110 --> 00:09:16.590
Make them cry, you know?

00:09:16.590 --> 00:09:21.670
I remember walking out of the theater and
the doors in Westwood…I was walking down

00:09:21.670 --> 00:09:24.680
the street past the theater and the doors
and people started to steam out and it [the

00:09:24.680 --> 00:09:31.360
movie] had just broken and I saw somebody
I knew but there were a lot of people between

00:09:31.360 --> 00:09:39.710
us and I sort of indicated…we waived hello
and then I pointed to the marquee and shrugged

00:09:39.710 --> 00:09:41.270
meaning “So what did you think?”

00:09:41.270 --> 00:09:45.840
and at this point he was able to get up to
me and said “….it’s….a….yeah…a

00:09:45.840 --> 00:09:50.490
worthy…movie I think you should see.”

00:09:50.490 --> 00:09:58.860
And I said like I said to you earlier (and
this does not make me very popular with people

00:09:58.860 --> 00:10:00.610
when I tell them what THEY thought of a movie.

00:10:00.610 --> 00:10:03.720
Or they tell me what they thought of a movie
and I tell them “No, that’s not what you

00:10:03.720 --> 00:10:04.830
thought.

00:10:04.830 --> 00:10:06.650
What you thought was such and such.”

00:10:06.650 --> 00:10:10.970
I understand that this is pretty arrogant
but what can I tell you?

00:10:10.970 --> 00:10:14.310
I’m just reporting honestly, my own reaction.

00:10:14.310 --> 00:10:20.931
I said to this guy “I hear you saying it’s
a worthy movie but in my impression it seems

00:10:20.931 --> 00:10:23.560
like you didn't like this movie.”

00:10:23.560 --> 00:10:29.390
Now I want you to imagine that instead of
the picture breaking and people streaming

00:10:29.390 --> 00:10:35.610
out, they are all crying, everybody is sobbing
with tears streaming down their faces.

00:10:35.610 --> 00:10:38.530
Well you wouldn’t want to see that movie
would you?

00:10:38.530 --> 00:10:39.620
The hell you wouldn’t!

00:10:39.620 --> 00:10:41.510
You’d immediately go to see that movie.

00:10:41.510 --> 00:10:42.880
You’d stop.

00:10:42.880 --> 00:10:46.460
You’d stand up your date or your next appointment
and you’d get in line to see that movie

00:10:46.460 --> 00:10:50.140
if it could affect people so strongly.

